Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["$GPRMC", "InlineCode"],
 " date field ",
 StyleBox["070313", "InlineCode"],
 " dates the fix to 2013-03-07 21:03:41 UTC, the same day the 2013 notebook \
was started. The ellipsoidal height H + N",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["", "g"]]],
 " = 350.361 m equals the altitude in the (truncated) ",
 StyleBox["$POLYP", "InlineCode"],
 " sentence, 350.361 m, which is the receiver's ellipsoid-referenced fix. The \
same fix appears in Table 2 as Ant-3: 22.716416\[Prime] and 49.877639\[Prime] \
(GGA 22.716417\[Prime], 49.877638\[Prime], i.e. 2 mm apart), with 350.346 m, \
15 mm below the POLYP value. So the ",
 StyleBox["Table 2 altitudes are WGS84 ellipsoidal heights",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", which is what the WGS84 transform needs."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4. Site inputs and antenna \[RightArrow] detector offsets", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4.1 Antenna coordinates (2014 note, Table 2)", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
Longitudes are entered with their physical sign (west negative), so the ECEF \
vectors below are real positions. The 2013 notebook entered them as positive. \
Distances are unchanged by that reflection, and \[Section]10 checks this. \
Table 2 marks the minutes of the Ash River coordinates with \[DoublePrime]. \
They are arc-minutes (degrees + decimal minutes).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ashAnt", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"{", "\n", "   ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"<|", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\"\<name\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Ash River Ant-1\>\""}], 
       ",", " ", 
